A||||5||DaveL||2.5 With Probable "Thrown" Rod||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||08-10-2001||08:19 AM||||OK, so my first 87 2.5 more than likely threw a rod, definitely "dead" untill I get next years tax refund. I've been wrestling with the question of whether to rebuild the old one if it is rebuildable at all, or find a used motor to plug in. My question is this, there is obvious coolant mixed in the oil in the crankcase. Will this cause unrepairable damage to the old block, if left in? Is there anything I should do if I want to keep the option of rebuilding this block? Drain oil, etc.....||||reg||5
Z||000001||TylerB11||08-10-2001||09:12 AM||||Oooh..I had an Outboard engine throw a rod...what an ugly mess...right into the junkyard.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-10-2001||09:45 AM||||Friend of mine just rebuilt a 2.5 Duke in a 1985 Ciera.  Said he found just about <B>everything</B> in that engine was worn - like it more or less "went" at once.  To me, that says rebuilding a Duke <B>might</B> tend to be expensive and time-consuming. <P>That's just one data point, of course, so it may not be represntative.  And I have never rebuilt a Duke, probably never will.  But since 2.5's are so common, I'm guessing that in many cases, it might be a good idea to find another and install it.  Or, use this as an opportunity to "move up" to another engine.<P>...Unless you're "in love" with this engine, or really want the experience of rebuilding it - that's what my friend did - he told me he did it just for something to do.<P>All of the above is just my humble opinion, of course.  Whatever you decide, best of luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||starfighter007@msn.com||08-10-2001||11:33 AM||||why not look into replaceing it with a auto zone rebuild with a warranty?I dont know what they get for there motors. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||08-10-2001||01:01 PM||||If you search the archives I think you'll find a thread from a couple of months back about a rebuilder selling reman 2.5's for a good price.  I don't remember if they were complete engines or just short blocks.<P>A used engine is almost always a pig in a poke, there's a real risk of getting a marginal or bad engine, but you're dealing with someone reputable, the price reflects the risk, and they'll warranty the engine it's a good way to go.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-10-2001||06:55 PM||||just get another motor from an 87+ Olds Cutlass Calais or Grand Am or several others. These aren't hard to find used in decent shape. Messing with a rod beat motor isn't worth the time.||||reg||1
